* {{IPA|de|[ˈla̝(ʔ)m̩]|a=Northern Germany}}
* {{IPA|de|[ˈlɑp.pən]|a=Swiss}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|de|gmh|lappe}}, from {{inh|de|goh|lappo}}, from {{der|de|gmw-pro|*lappā|t=cloth, rag, lobe}} of {{unc|de|nocap=1}} origin, possibly {{der|de|ine-pro|*leb-||to hang loosely}}.<ref>{{R:Online_Etymology_Dictionary|lap}}</ref><ref>{{R:es:Roberts:2014|title=lapa}}</ref>

The form is remarkable because of the unshifted geminate ''-pp-'' (instead of expected Old High German *''lapfo''). Based on the supposedly invariable nature of the consonant shift, {{m|goh||lappo}} would have to be an originally [[Central German|Central]] and [[Low German]] form (compare {{cog|osx|lappo}}). This is, however, quite unlikely since the Old High German word is attested as early as the 9th century, and no variants with ''-pf-'' exist. The form remains thus unexplained.

